.\" -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
.TH CppDataSet 3 "" "CppDatasetUtil" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
CppDataSet \- recursive Dataset value with optional comments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <Dataset.h>
.B class Dataset;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Dataset holds null, bool, a signed 64-bit integer, double, string, map or
array. Map and array elements are also Dataset values. Each value may have
its own comments. Maps are sorted by key and arrays preserve their order.
Copying and moving preserve both values and comments.
.SH TYPES AND CONSTRUCTORS
.B Dataset::Map
is std::map<std::string, Dataset>,
.B Dataset::Array
is std::vector<Dataset>, and
.B Dataset::Value
is a std::variant of the supported types. The default constructor creates null
without comments. Constructors also accept nullptr, bool, int, std::int64_t,
double, const char*, std::string, Map and Array. A null const char* throws
std::invalid_argument.
.PP
Any other integer type, such as long long, std::size_t or unsigned, is
stored as a signed 64-bit integer; an unsigned value above the int64 range
throws std::out_of_range. Pointers other than const char* are rejected at
compile time instead of becoming bool.
.SH ACCESS
.TP
.B operator[](const std::string& key)
Accesses a map entry. A mutable null value becomes an empty map, and a
missing key is inserted with a null value. The parent value's comments are
preserved. Const access changes nothing and throws std::out_of_range if the
key is missing. Access to a non-map value throws std::logic_error.
.TP
.B operator[](std::size_t index)
Accesses an array element without inserting it. Indices start at zero. A
non-array value throws std::logic_error; an out-of-range index throws
std::out_of_range. Replacing the parent Dataset invalidates references to
its elements.
.TP
.B value() const noexcept
Returns a const reference to Value. On a type mismatch, std::get throws
std::bad_variant_access and std::get_if returns a null pointer.
.TP
.B size() const noexcept
Returns the number of entries of a map or array, and 0 for any other value.
.TP
.B contains(const std::string& key) const noexcept
Returns true if the value is a map that contains key.
.TP
.B push_back(Dataset value)
Appends an element to an array. A null value first becomes an empty array
and keeps its comments. Any other type throws std::logic_error.
.TP
.B erase(const std::string& key)
Removes a map entry and returns whether it existed. A non-map value throws
std::logic_error.
.TP
.B erase(std::size_t index)
Removes an array element and shifts later elements down. A non-array value
throws std::logic_error; an out-of-range index throws std::out_of_range.
.TP
.B value() noexcept
Returns a mutable reference to Value. Assigning a new alternative replaces
the value and keeps the comments.
.TP
.B operator==, operator!=
Compare values and comments recursively. A NaN is never equal to itself.
.SH COMMENTS
.TP
.B comments() const noexcept
Returns a const reference to the comments in their stored order.
.TP
.B set_comments(std::vector<std::string> comments)
Replaces the comments. Specify text without a leading #; embedded line breaks
produce multiple comment lines. YAML and TOML require valid UTF-8 and reject
unsupported control characters and Unicode line separators. TOML comments
that cannot be placed syntactically are omitted. CSV and JSON do not save
comments. Assignment or a successful Load also replaces existing comments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B Load(const std::string& path)
Loads CSV, JSON, YAML or TOML based on the case-insensitive extension .csv,
\&.json, .yaml, .yml or .toml. Comments are not loaded. Success replaces the
value and comments; failure leaves the Dataset unchanged. I/O errors, invalid
input, and unsupported extensions or values throw std::runtime_error.
.TP
.B "static Parse(const std::string& text, const std::string& format = ""json"")"
Parses CSV, JSON, YAML or TOML text. format is a case-insensitive name: csv,
json, yaml, yml or toml (default json). The rules, including the nesting
limit, are those of Load, without comments. Use it to expand the string kept
for a value nested deeper than the limit. Invalid input or an unsupported
format throws std::runtime_error.
.TP
.B "static LoadDirectory(const std::string& path, bool recursive = false)"
Returns a std::list of path and Dataset pairs sorted by path. Subdirectories
are traversed only when recursive=true. Unsupported extensions are ignored.
Symbolic links to files and directories are followed; a target reached by
multiple paths is loaded once per path. Returned paths retain the supplied
directory and link names. A file link's own extension determines its format.
Cycles, broken links, unreadable entries and failed files are skipped with a
warning on stderr. A starting directory that cannot be opened throws
std::runtime_error. Comments are not loaded.
.TP
.B Save(const std::string& path) const
Saves according to the extension. CSV requires an array of nonempty maps
with identical keys and string values; an empty array produces an empty file.
The TOML root must be a map and null is unsupported. Non-finite floating-point
numbers cannot be saved. YAML and TOML save representable comments.
The content is written to a temporary file and renamed over the target, so a
write error leaves an existing file unchanged. Symbolic links are followed and
permissions are kept. I/O errors, unsupported extensions, and values or
comments that cannot be represented throw std::runtime_error.
.PP
CSV uses the first row as column names and loads each subsequent row as a
map of strings. Duplicate names and inconsistent field counts are errors.
YAML resolves supported core scalars and rejects multiple documents, duplicate
anchors, recursive aliases and unsupported tags. TOML date/time values and
non-finite numbers cannot be loaded.
.PP
Maps and arrays nested deeper than Dataset::kMaxNestingDepth (256) are not
loaded as trees. Each is kept as a string holding its compact JSON text,
which Parse converts back. YAML anchors cannot be defined inside such
values, TOML nested more than twice the limit is rejected, and Save rejects
values nested beyond the limit. YAML aliases that expand to more than
1,000,000 values in total are errors.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <Dataset.h>
#include <string>
#include <variant>
Dataset data;
data["server"]["host"] = "localhost";
data["server"]["host"].set_comments({"Server address"});
data.Save("config.yaml");
Dataset loaded;
loaded.Load("config.yaml");
const auto& host = std::get<std::string>(
    loaded["server"]["host"].value());
.fi
.SH DATA-DRIVEN TESTING
Run the following GoogleTest example from the project root. Dataset reads two
files in Tests/data/server-config; the independent ServerConfig model is the
test subject. Its endpoint and enabled state are compared with expected values.
The file-count and nonempty-case checks help detect skipped data files.
Each file contains cases with input and expected values:
.PP
.B Tests/data/server-config/basic.json
.nf
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {"host": "localhost", "port": 8080, "enabled": true},
      "expected": {"endpoint": "localhost:8080", "enabled": true}
    },
    {
      "input": {"host": "127.0.0.1", "port": 0, "enabled": false},
      "expected": {"endpoint": "127.0.0.1:0", "enabled": false}
    }
  ]
}
.fi
.PP
.B Tests/data/server-config/alternate.toml
.nf
[[cases]]
[cases.input]
host = "example.net"
port = 443
enabled = false

[cases.expected]
endpoint = "example.net:443"
enabled = false
.fi
.PP
.nf
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <variant>

#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include "Dataset.h"

class ServerConfig {
 public:
  ServerConfig(std::string host, std::int64_t port, bool enabled)
      : host_(std::move(host)), port_(port), enabled_(enabled) {}

  std::string endpoint() const { return host_ + ":" + std::to_string(port_); }
  bool enabled() const { return enabled_; }

 private:
  std::string host_;
  std::int64_t port_;
  bool enabled_;
};

TEST(ServerConfigTest, BuildsEndpointAndStoresEnabledState) {
  const auto files = Dataset::LoadDirectory("Tests/data/server-config");
  ASSERT_EQ(files.size(), 2u);
  for (const auto& [path, dataset] : files) {
    SCOPED_TRACE(path);
    const auto& cases = std::get<Dataset::Array>(dataset["cases"].value());
    ASSERT_FALSE(cases.empty());
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cases.size(); ++i) {
      SCOPED_TRACE(i);
      const Dataset& input = cases[i]["input"];
      const Dataset& expected = cases[i]["expected"];
      const ServerConfig server(std::get<std::string>(input["host"].value()),
                                std::get<std::int64_t>(input["port"].value()),
                                std::get<bool>(input["enabled"].value()));
      EXPECT_EQ(server.endpoint(),
                std::get<std::string>(expected["endpoint"].value()));
      EXPECT_EQ(server.enabled(), std::get<bool>(expected["enabled"].value()));
    }
  }
}
.fi
